%[text] # Design Solid State Transformer Power Delivery for Hyperscale Data Center
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"**Table of Contents**"}
%[text]
%[text] This example shows how to model and validate a Solid State Transformer (SST) power delivery system for a hyperscale data center rack. The SST uses a Cascaded H-Bridge (CHB) to Dual Active Bridge (DAB) topology to step down 13.8 kV medium-voltage AC directly to 800 V DC. This approach eliminates bulky low-frequency transformers.
%[text] Key features of this architecture:
%[text] - Direct MV-to-LV conversion through power electronics (no 60 Hz transformer)
%[text] - Modular SST design: 3 phases x 3 H-bridge modules per phase = 9 DAB converters
%[text] - 800 V DC bus directly serving GPU racks with per-rack Battery Backup Units (BBU)
%[text] - Hybrid BBU (supercapacitor + battery) for transient voltage support \
%[text] ## System Architecture
%[text]  ![](text:image:5caf)1
%[text] The power delivery path is:
%[text] - 13.8 kV AC grid (3-phase, 60 Hz) feeds an RL filter for harmonic attenuation
%[text] - CHB Phase Legs (3 phases, 3 series H-bridge modules each) rectify to 4400 V DC
%[text] - 9 DAB converters provide galvanic isolation and step down to 800 V DC
%[text] - 800 V DC bus supplies two GPU racks (training + inference workloads)
%[text] - Hybrid BBU (supercapacitor for ms-range, battery for sec-range) on each rack \
%[text] Each GPU rack contains 4 physical trays bundled as one logical unit:
%[text] - 4 IT trays x 576 GPUs = 2304 GPUs per logical rack
%[text] - GPU Thermal Design Power (TDP): 2500 W, idle: 375 W
%[text] - Power-utilization follows Fan/Barroso model: P = idle + (TDP-idle) x (2U - U^1.4)
%[text] - Per-tray power range: 0.33 MW (idle) to 1.68 MW (full load) \
%[text] **Top-Level Model:**
%[text]  ![](text:image:21fb)
%%
%[text] ## Solid State Transformer (SST) Detail
%[text] The SST is the core power conversion stage. It replaces a conventional bulky 60 Hz transformer with a high-frequency power electronics-based approach. This achieves higher power density and controllability.
%[text] For a full project overview, open the [project presentation](matlab:web('presentation.html','-browser')).
%[text] **SST Subsystem:**
%[text] The SST subsystem contains:
%[text] - **RL Input Filter** - 10 mH / 0.1 Ohm per phase for harmonic attenuation
%[text] - **PFC Rectifier Controller** - dq-frame current control with DC voltage regulation
%[text] - **DC Voltage Balancing** - ensures equal voltage sharing across CHB modules
%[text] - **Converters** - 3 CHB phase legs, each containing 3 H-bridge + DAB modules \
%[text] ![](text:image:2640)
%%
%[text] ## Cascaded H-Bridge (CHB) and DAB Converters
%[text] Each phase leg consists of 3 series-connected H-bridge modules. Each module:
%[text] - Rectifies its share of the AC voltage onto a 4400 V DC bus capacitor
%[text] - Feeds a Dual Active Bridge (DAB) converter for galvanic isolation
%[text] - Steps down from 4400 V to 800 V DC via the DAB \
%[text] The modular structure provides:
%[text] - Voltage sharing across lower-rated semiconductor devices
%[text] - Redundancy (N+1 capability with bypass)
%[text] - Independent voltage regulation per module via DAB Proportional-Integral (PI) control
%[text] - **Converters Subsystem (3 Phase Legs)** \
%[text]  ![](text:image:0121)
%%
%[text] ## DAB Converter Module
%[text] Each DAB module implements a bidirectional DC-DC converter through a generalized average model (switching-frequency dynamics without individual switching events). Key features:
%[text] - Switching frequency: 20 kHz
%[text] - Turns ratio and leakage inductance provide voltage step-down
%[text] - All leakage referred to the primary side (secondary leakage = 0)
%[text] - PI controller regulates 800 V output by adjusting the phase shift between primary and secondary bridges \
%%
%[text] ## PFC Rectifier Controller
%[text] The PFC (Power Factor Correction) controller operates in the synchronous dq reference frame to achieve:
%[text] - Unity power factor on the grid side (Id tracks power demand, Iq = 0)
%[text] - DC bus voltage regulation at 4400 V per module
%[text] - Decoupled d-axis and q-axis current control \
%[text] The CHB phase legs use an internal modulation scaling factor (computed from grid voltage and total DC bus voltage). The external `Gain_mod_scale` block is set to 1 — all scaling is handled inside the custom Simscape component.
%%
%[text] ## Server Load and GPU Racks
%[text] The 800 V DC bus feeds two logical racks representing distinct AI workloads:
%[text] - **Training Rack** - large-batch gradient computations, high sustained utilization
%[text] - **Inference Rack** - token generation with prefill/decode/eviction cycles \
%[text] Each logical rack aggregates 4 physical trays (2304 GPUs total). GPU power follows the Fan/Barroso model, where power scales nonlinearly with utilization. At 50% utilization, the GPU draws 70% of peak power due to leakage currents and clock distribution overhead.
%%
%[text] ## Battery Backup Unit (BBU)
%[text] Each rack has a hybrid BBU for transient voltage support:
%[text] - **Supercapacitor** - fast response (microseconds), handles millisecond-range transients via proportional droop control.
%[text] - **Battery** - slower response (milliseconds), handles second-range events via PI control. \
%[text] The BBU uses a **complementary filter** design:
%[text] - **Normal operation:** Dead zone keeps both BBU elements completely silent. The central DAB controller handles all load-driven voltage transients (max dip is well below the dead zone threshold).
%[text] - **Fault/island:** When bus voltage deviates beyond the dead zone, the High-Pass Filter (HPF) path routes fast transients to the supercap (P-only, immediate burst). The Low-Pass Filter (LPF) path routes sustained errors to the battery PI controller. The complementary split (HPF = input - LPF) guarantees no controller fighting. Because the supercap has no integrator, only the battery owns steady-state authority. \
%[text] **Role in this architecture:**
%[text] - Cases 1-4 demonstrate SST sufficiency (BBU silent via dead zone).
%[text] - Case 5 demonstrates localized BBU ride-through when a rack-level breaker opens (coordinated supercap + battery response).
%[text] - Case 6 validates SST grid-level Low Voltage Ride-Through (LVRT) using a representative Electric Reliability Council of Texas (ERCOT) ascending voltage profile \[7\]. \
%[text] **Battery Energy Storage Subsystem:**
%%
%[text] ## Model Parameters
%[text] The model is parameterized through HyperscaleDataCenterParam`.m` which calls:
%[text] - `SSTParam.m` - SST topology, CHB/DAB/filter/PFC controller parameters
%[text] - `BBUParam.m` - Hybrid BBU gains, time constants, and limits
%[text] - `UPSBESSParam.m` - Additional energy storage parameters \
%[text] ## Simulation Scenarios
%[text] Initialize model and parameters. Cases 1-4 use a dead zone so the BBU remains silent during all normal load transients. Case 5 opens a rack-level breaker to demonstrate localized BBU ride-through with coordinated supercap (HPF) and battery (LPF+PI) response. Case 6 applies a representative ERCOT ascending LVRT staircase \[7\] to the grid source.
%[text] Reload the model fresh to ensure signal logging is intact after browsing subsystems.
modelName = 'HyperscaleDataCenter';
load_system(modelName);
clear sst bbu;
HyperscaleDataCenterParam;
stopTime = '10';
%%
%[text] ## Case 1: Mixed Workload — SST Dynamic Response
%[text] **Scenario:** Training rack runs a periodic 4-stage profile (idle/forward/backward/comm) from the Normalized LLM Resource (NLR) paper \[2\]. Inference rack runs request-driven prefill/decode cycles from the roofline model.
%[text] **Purpose:** Verify that the DAB PI controller maintains voltage quality under realistic, rapidly-varying GPU workloads without BBU assistance. Power-utilization correlation validates the Fan/Barroso model.
%[text] **Grid stability concern:** This case also characterizes the grid-side power oscillations created by synchronized training phases — a critical power stabilization challenge identified by Choukse et al. \[5\]. During bulk-synchronous training, all GPUs alternate between compute (U=0.92, near TDP) and communication (U=0.15, near idle) every ~800 ms. This creates a periodic power swing at 1.25 Hz, directly in the 1-2.5 Hz critical band where inter-plant generator oscillations occur. At datacenter scale (100k+ GPUs), these swings reach hundreds of MW. This can excite Sub-Synchronous Resonance (SSR) in turbine shafts and transmission lines \[5\].
%[text] **What to look for:** Power correctly tracks utilization (high correlation). DC bus voltage stays within +/-5% band despite rapid load transitions. Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) of grid power reveals training-step harmonics in critical frequency bands (\<1 Hz, 1-2.5 Hz, 7+ Hz).

HyperscaleDataCenterParam;
MixedWorkload;
simOutCase1 = sim(modelName, 'StopTime', stopTime);
m = extractVdcMetrics(simOutCase1);
caseMetrics(1) = struct('name',"Mixed Workload (Realistic)", ...
    'Vdc_mean',m.Vdc_mean, 'Vdc_std',m.Vdc_std, 'Vdc_pp',m.Vdc_pp, ...
    'bbuVerdict',"SST SUFFICIENT");

%
% Scatter plot validates power-utilization correlation against Fan/Barroso curve.
hsplot.powerVsUtilization(simOutCase1); %[output:31d7a97f]

%
% GPU utilization profiles show distinct training and inference patterns.
hsplot.utilization(simOutCase1); %[output:158a10b8]

%
% Power flow with shaded fills shows how each rack's demand varies.
hsplot.powerFlow(simOutCase1, [2.5 4.0]); %[output:6f1adaa7]

%
% DC bus voltage with +-5% band during mixed workload.
hsplot.dcBusVoltage(simOutCase1, [2.5 4.0]); %[output:65593e02]

%
%[text] ### Grid Power Swing Analysis
%[text] The grid power swing analysis below quantifies this stabilization challenge for the current model parameters. The synchronized training transitions create large power oscillations visible on the 13.8 kV AC grid side — the same phenomenon observed in production GPU training clusters \[5, Fig. 1\].
%[text] The FFT reveals frequency content concentrated in critical bands that can excite grid infrastructure resonances \[5, Sec. III-B; 6\]:
%[text] - **\<1 Hz**: Transmission line inter-area oscillations (North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) documented modes with damping ratios near unity \[6\])
%[text] - **1-2.5 Hz**: Inter-plant coupled generator oscillations (units in the same plant or nearby plants)
%[text] - **7+ Hz**: Turbine-generator shaft torsional critical frequencies (HP/reheat/LP stage mechanical resonances \[5\]) \
%[text] The dominant peak at ~1.25 Hz corresponds directly to the 800 ms training step period (1/0.8 s = 1.25 Hz), with harmonics at 2.5 Hz and 3.75 Hz spreading energy across multiple critical bands. The 1.25 Hz lines marked on the FFT plot are the training step fundamental and its harmonics.
swingMetrics = hsplot.gridPowerSwings(simOutCase1); %[output:0f1c3b5d]

%
%[text] ### Power Swing Summary
%[text] At the single-SST scale (4608 GPUs), the grid power swing is already significant. Scaled to a full 100,000-GPU training cluster, these oscillations would reach hundreds of MW - matching industry observations of datacenter-induced grid instability \[5\].
disp(buildSwingTable(swingMetrics)) %[output:96d07fad]

%
%[text] ### Ramp Rate Compliance
%[text] Utility operators limit how fast load can ramp \[5, Sec. III-A\]. The actual ramp rate limit varies by utility and region. The plot below shows the measured ramp rate distribution against a user-specified limit. Exceedances beyond the spec require mitigation (GPU power smoothing, energy storage, or software load shaping).
rampRateLimit = 5;  % Utility-specific — adjust to actual spec
hsplot.rampRateCompliance(simOutCase1, rampRateLimit); %[output:5583cab9]
%%
%[text] ## Case 2: Synchronized Load Step — SST Transient Response
%[text] **Scenario:** Both racks step simultaneously from 10% idle at t=3.0 s. Training transitions into realistic cycling stages (fwd 85% / bwd 92% / comm 15%), while inference steps to a moderate steady 45%. This represents a coordinated burst with asymmetric demand — more realistic than uniform 100% across both racks.
%[text] **Purpose:** Stress-test the DAB PI controller's transient response under a large coordinated step with different post-step profiles on each rack. The output capacitance and PI loop must absorb the transient without BBU assistance, then maintain regulation as training cycles through stages.
%[text] **What to look for:** Voltage dip magnitude after the step, settling time back to 800 V, dip stays within +/-5% band (760-840 V), and continued regulation quality during post-step cycling.
HyperscaleDataCenterParam;
SynchronizedLoadStep;
simOutCase2 = sim(modelName, 'StopTime', stopTime);
m = extractVdcMetrics(simOutCase2);
caseMetrics(2) = struct('name',"Synchronized Step (10%→cycling)", ...
    'Vdc_mean',m.Vdc_mean, 'Vdc_std',m.Vdc_std, 'Vdc_pp',m.Vdc_pp, ...
    'bbuVerdict',"SST SUFFICIENT");

%
% GPU utilization shows the asymmetric step: training into cycling stages, inference to steady 45%.
hsplot.utilization(simOutCase2); %[output:5eb3cd10]

%
% DC bus voltage during the step event — shows dip and recovery.
hsplot.dcBusVoltage(simOutCase2, [2.8 3.5]); %[output:6782f999]

%
% DC bus voltage and current detail around the step.
hsplot.dcBusVI(simOutCase2, [2.9 3.2]); %[output:82b47ba1]

%
% Power flow shows the asymmetric step — training ramps into cycling, inference to steady.
hsplot.powerFlow(simOutCase2, [2.9 3.2]); %[output:5484c48f]
%%
%[text] ## Case 3: Load Shedding — SST Overvoltage Handling
%[text] **Scenario:** Both racks operating at 90% utilization suddenly drop to 5% at t=3.0 s. This represents an emergency power reduction (large negative step).
%[text] **Purpose:** Verify that the DAB PI controller limits overvoltage on the 800V DC bus during a large negative load step without BBU assistance.
%[text] **What to look for:** DC bus overshoot should remain within +/-5% band (\< 840 V) using only the DAB PI regulation.
HyperscaleDataCenterParam;
LoadShedding;
simOutCase3 = sim(modelName, 'StopTime', stopTime);
m = extractVdcMetrics(simOutCase3);
caseMetrics(3) = struct('name',"Load Shedding (90%→5%)", ...
    'Vdc_mean',m.Vdc_mean, 'Vdc_std',m.Vdc_std, 'Vdc_pp',m.Vdc_pp, ...
    'bbuVerdict',"SST SUFFICIENT");

%
% DC bus voltage shows transient overshoot then recovery (no BBU).
hsplot.dcBusVI(simOutCase3, [2.9 3.2]); %[output:8491144f]

%
% Power flow shows the sharp drop from full load to idle.
hsplot.powerFlow(simOutCase3, [2.9 3.2]); %[output:97a2232a]

%
% DC bus voltage zoomed on the shedding event.
hsplot.dcBusVoltage(simOutCase3, [2.9 3.2]); %[output:4ab08db8]
%%
%[text] ## Case 4: Unbalanced Load Ramp — SST Phase Balancing
%[text] **Scenario:** Training rack ramps from 10% to 95% utilization over 1 second starting at t=3.0 s, while the inference rack stays at 10%. This creates a severe load imbalance between the two racks.
%[text] **Purpose:** This scenario uniquely stresses two SST capabilities that a conventional transformer-rectifier system cannot provide:
%[text] - *Unity power factor across a wide operating range* — the PFC controller continuously adjusts the dq-frame current references as load varies. A passive diode rectifier would have poor power factor at light load with uncontrolled reactive power swings.
%[text] - *Per-phase DC voltage balancing* — with unequal load on the 800 V bus, the three CHB phase legs see slightly different power demands due to the single-phase H-bridge ripple interaction. The DC Voltage Balancing subsystem applies per-phase modulation corrections to keep all three phase DC buses at 4400 V despite the imbalance. \
%[text] **What to look for:**
%[text] - Grid voltage stays at 1.0 pu throughout (no load-dependent droop)
%[text] - DC bus voltage stays within +/-5% band during and after the ramp
%[text] - Power ramps smoothly on training rack while inference stays flat
%[text] - No oscillation or instability from the asymmetric loading \
HyperscaleDataCenterParam;
RapidLoadRamp;
simOutCase4 = sim(modelName, 'StopTime', stopTime);
m = extractVdcMetrics(simOutCase4);
caseMetrics(4) = struct('name',"Unbalanced Ramp (95%/10%)", ...
    'Vdc_mean',m.Vdc_mean, 'Vdc_std',m.Vdc_std, 'Vdc_pp',m.Vdc_pp, ...
    'bbuVerdict',"SST SUFFICIENT");

%
% Combined SST performance: grid voltage, power tracking, and DC bus
% regulation in one 3-panel view. This is the key evidence that the SST
% maintains control quality throughout the unbalanced ramp.
hsplot.sstPerformance(simOutCase4, [2.5 5.0], [1 inf], ["Ramp Start", "Ramp End"]); %[output:75bdd985]

%
% GPU utilization shows the asymmetry: training ramps while inference is flat.
hsplot.utilization(simOutCase4); %[output:765b24d8]

%
% Zoomed power flow during the ramp event — training rack ramps while inference stays constant.
hsplot.powerFlow(simOutCase4, [2.8 4.2]); %[output:830b65af]

%
% Grid-side measurements confirm stable voltage and smooth current ramp
% with no reactive power oscillation (evidence of unity PF).
hsplot.gridPower(simOutCase4, [2.8 4.2]); %[output:17c04289]

%
% DC bus voltage with +-5% band zoomed on the ramp period.
hsplot.dcBusVoltage(simOutCase4, [2.8 4.2]); %[output:06d48508]
%%
%[text] ## Case 5: Rack-Level Breaker Trip — Localized BBU Ride-Through
%[text] **Scenario:** The DC distribution breaker, a Single Pole Single Throw (SPST) switch, on the Training Rack opens at t=4.0 s during realistic mixed-workload operation. The training rack cycles through idle/forward/backward/comm stages (5-92% utilization). The inference rack runs prefill/decode batches. The grid and SST remain healthy — this is a local distribution fault, not a grid event.
%[text] **Purpose:** Demonstrate that the hybrid per-rack BBU (supercap + battery) can autonomously sustain the isolated DC bus at 800 V under time-varying load when it becomes the sole energy source. The supercap provides instant response, preventing Constant Power Load (CPL) instability. The battery then takes over for sustained ride-through, tracking the dynamic load without voltage collapse.
%[text] **Design:** The complementary filter BBU architecture uses a dead zone to remain silent during normal DAB-regulated operation (Cases 1-4). When the breaker opens, a fault flag bypasses the dead zone so the full voltage error drives the BBU immediately. The HPF path routes fast transients to the supercap (P-only, burst response) while the LPF path routes sustained errors to the battery PI controller. The battery string provides sufficient voltage headroom to deliver peak power during load stages. Bus capacitance is provided by the IT tray PSU input filters. The fault flag activates at the same instant as the breaker opens.
%[text] **What to look for:** Voltage stays within the +/-5% regulation band (760-840 V) throughout the island period. Supercap provides the initial transient burst (HPF), battery ramps to steady-state (LPF+PI). No controller fighting. The Inference Rack voltage is undisturbed.

HyperscaleDataCenterParam;
MixedWorkload;

% Breaker opens at t=4s — Training Rack only
set_param([modelName '/Server Load/Racks/PS Step'], 'step_time', '4');
% Fault flag activates BBU (bypasses dead zone) at same instant
set_param([modelName '/Server Load/Racks/Fault Flag'], 'Time', '4');

simOutCase5 = sim(modelName, 'StopTime', stopTime);

% Metrics: full ride-through window (breaker open to end of sim)
Vdc_rack = simOutCase5.logsout.get('VdcTrain').Values;
idxPost = Vdc_rack.Time >= 4.0;
vPost = double(Vdc_rack.Data(idxPost));
m = struct();  % Case 5 sets metrics directly (no extractVdcMetrics); ensure m is a fresh struct
m.Vdc_mean = mean(vPost);
m.Vdc_std = std(vPost);
m.Vdc_pp = max(vPost) - min(vPost);
caseMetrics(5) = struct('name',"Breaker Trip (Training Rack)", ...
    'Vdc_mean',m.Vdc_mean, 'Vdc_std',m.Vdc_std, 'Vdc_pp',m.Vdc_pp, ...
    'bbuVerdict',"BBU ISLAND");

% Reset breaker and fault flag for subsequent runs
set_param([modelName '/Server Load/Racks/PS Step'], 'step_time', '100');
set_param([modelName '/Server Load/Racks/Fault Flag'], 'Time', '100');

%
% GPU utilization — realistic mixed workload (training stages + inference batches).
hsplot.utilization(simOutCase5); %[output:13cd51c7]

%
% Power flow — Training Rack power supplied by BBU after trip; inference continues from grid.
hsplot.powerFlow(simOutCase5, [3.8 4.5]); %[output:5cf0ac62]

%
% Grid-side measurements — grid voltage stable, DC bus current from grid drops at trip.
hsplot.gridPower(simOutCase5, [3.8 4.5]); %[output:6025c744]

%
% Training Rack local voltage vs main bus — BBU sustains islanded rack voltage.
hsplot.breakerRideThrough(simOutCase5, [3.8 4.5]); %[output:44b8db08]

%
% BBU current handoff — supercap provides initial burst (HPF), battery ramps in (LPF).
hsplot.bbuHandoff(simOutCase5, [4.0 4.2]); %[output:63646689]

%
% ITIC/CBEMA compliance — load voltage assessed against IT equipment tolerance envelope.
hsplot.iticCompliance(simOutCase5, 4.0); %[output:5b6dd50c]
%%
%[text] ## Case 6: Grid LVRT — SST Ride-Through with Representative ERCOT Ascending Profile
%[text] **Scenario:** The utility grid experiences a low-voltage event following a representative ERCOT ascending staircase profile \[7\] (near-zero to full recovery over ~3 s). The SST must maintain 800 V DC bus regulation at the load throughout the event, while racks operate with the same realistic mixed workload as Case 1 (NLR 4-stage training + roofline inference).
%[text] **Purpose:** Validate SST ride-through capability across a representative ERCOT LVRT voltage range \[7\]. The ascending profile tests progressively easier operating points — from deep fault through partial recovery to full restoration. The Battery Energy Storage System (BESS) supplements when grid voltage is too low for the SST to deliver sufficient power, and the SST takes over as voltage recovers.
%[text] **Design:** The CHB front-end can extract current from the grid at reduced voltage (limited by thermal rating). Below a threshold grid voltage the available grid power is insufficient and the BESS supplements. Above this threshold, the SST PFC controller adjusts Id current to maintain power balance. The DAB stage is decoupled and continues regulating 800 V regardless of upstream grid voltage.
%[text] **What to look for:** 800 V bus stays within +/-5% (760-840 V) across all ERCOT voltage steps. CHB intermediate bus (4400 V) shows transients at each step but recovers. Information Technology Industry Council (ITIC) compliance confirms IT equipment tolerance is never exceeded. The ERCOT boundary plot shows which ranges the SST handles vs where datacenter equipment is required.
HyperscaleDataCenterParam;
SSTRideThrough;

simOutCase6 = sim(modelName, 'StopTime', stopTime);

% Metrics
m = extractVdcMetrics(simOutCase6, lvrt.startTime);
caseMetrics(6) = struct('name',"ERCOT LVRT Ride-Through", ...
    'Vdc_mean',m.Vdc_mean, 'Vdc_std',m.Vdc_std, 'Vdc_pp',m.Vdc_pp, ...
    'bbuVerdict',"SST+BESS");

%
% ERCOT voltage profile — shows staircase with SST vs datacenter equipment zones.
hsplot.lvrtRangeDefinition(lvrtGridCode, lvrt.startTime); %[output:0936e6a5]

%
% OCP LVRT power balance — grid power (SST) and BESS power with Range 1/2 annotations.
hsplot.lvrtPowerBalance(simOutCase6, lvrtGridCode, lvrt.startTime); %[output:50db2aba]

%
% LVRT performance — grid voltage, 800V bus, CHB intermediate bus during sag.
sagRange = [lvrt.startTime, lvrt.startTime + 6.0];
hsplot.lvrtPerformance(simOutCase6, sagRange); %[output:91cf7853]

%
% DC bus voltage — 800V regulation quality with OCP +/-3% spec limits.
hsplot.dcBusVoltage(simOutCase6, [lvrt.startTime, lvrt.startTime + 2.0]); %[output:460a4f6c]

%
% ITIC/CBEMA compliance — load voltage vs IT equipment tolerance envelope.
hsplot.iticCompliance(simOutCase6, lvrt.startTime); %[output:9fc0f0f3]
%%
%[text] ## Summary
%[text] The table below summarizes the DC bus voltage regulation metrics and BBU status across all simulation cases. Cases 1-4 evaluate SST performance under various load transients. Case 5 tests localized BBU ride-through during a rack-level breaker trip. Case 6 validates SST grid-level LVRT capability using a representative ERCOT ascending voltage profile \[7\].
summaryTable = buildSummaryTable(caseMetrics);
disp(summaryTable) %[output:861a8a98]
%%
%[text] ## References
%[text] 1. NVIDIA, "NVIDIA Kyber NVL576 Architecture," 2025.
%[text] 2. Vercellino et al., "NLR: Characterizing LLM Training Power at Scale," 2024.
%[text] 3. Niu et al., "TokenPowerBench: Benchmarking LLM Inference Energy," 2024.
%[text] 4. Fan, Weber, and Barroso, "Power Provisioning for a Warehouse-sized Computer," ISCA, 2007.
%[text] 5. Choukse et al., "Power Stabilization for AI Training Datacenters," Microsoft/OpenAI/NVIDIA, arXiv:2508.14318, 2025.
%[text] 6. NERC, "Interconnection Oscillation Analysis," Reliability Assessment Technical Report, July 2019.
%[text] 7. Open Compute Project, "Solid State Transformer Specification," Revision 0.3.0, Google/Microsoft/NVIDIA, June 2026.
%[text] 8.  G. Pugliese, F. Castellone, M. Liserre, and G. De Carne, "Active Power Balancing Technique for Series-Connected Modular Solid-State Transformers," *IEEE Trans. Power Electron.*, vol. 32, no. 8, pp. 5934–5944, Aug. 2017.
%[text] 9. H. Qin and J. W. Kimball, "Generalized Average Modeling of Dual Active Bridge DC–DC Converter," *IEEE Trans. Power Electron.*, vol. 27, no. 4, pp. 2078–2084, Apr. 2012. \
